OVERVIEW:

 This program generates and verifies "magic squares," which are squares of size
 NxN where every row, column, and diagonal all sum to the same number.

INCLUDED FILES:

 * README - The file you're in now! Explains various behaviours users can expect
   of the program.
 * MagicSquareDriver.java - The program that the user interfaces with to
   create/check magic squares.
 * MagicSquare.java - Primary logic holder for reading/writing/generating magic
   squares.
 * MagicSquareInterface.java - Definition for what a magic square implementation
   should be able to do.
 * MagicSquareTest.java - Meant for developers to be able to test any changes
   they've made. Disregard if you are a normal user.
 * (in)validNxN - Various different files containing example magic square files.
   Primarily used for developer testing purposes. Disregard if you are a normal
   user.

COMPILING AND RUNNING:

 From the directory containing the source files (and this file you're currently
 reading), execute the following command to compile the source code into a
 binary you can run:

 $ javac *.java

 Following this, you can execute the code using this command:

 $ java MagicSquareDriver

 This command should print out a message detailing how you can use the program
 to actually check/create magic squares.

PROGRAM DESIGN AND IMPORTANT CONCEPTS:

 MagicSquareDriver.java is the actual entrypoint into the program. This serves
 largely as a way to intercept user input, sanity check that input, and then
 call out to an actual magic square implementation (located elsewhere) to give
 the user what they want.

 The actual logic for what it means to be a magic square is located in
 MagicSquareInterface.java. Defining a ubiquitous interface for a magic square
 doesn't serve any meaningful purpose other than challenging the author (me) to
 use an interface. The business logic for the magic square implementation lies
 in MagicSquare.java, where the "how" of checking/creating a magic square, as
 well as reading and writing files for magic squares is handled.

TESTING:

 First and foremost, the functionality of the logic defined in MagicSquare.java
 was ensured to have passed all the tests supplied in MagicSquareTest.java.
 Additionally, the program was test by hand with several faulty inputs to ensure
 that an inane request wasn't allowed further than the input sanitization step.
 If the user supplies 0 arguments, 1 argument, 1 correct argument (-check or
 -create) with too few arguments beyond that, too many arguments beyond that, or
 nonsensical arguments beyond that (e.g. a floating point square size), a simple
 usage message is printed out and the program exits.

 Furthermore, if the user tries to do things resulting in an error that can't be
 checked during input sanitization, the error is propagated and returned back to
 the user. For example, if the user tries to check a file that doesn't exist, a
 FileNotFoundException is thrown and crashes the program.

DISCUSSION:

 I wouldn't say the programming itself was very difficult, as it was mostly a
 review of things I've learned in previous classes/experiences. I did have to
 look up a few things just to remind myself of a couple menial tasks, such as
 parsing a String as an int [1], but overall the programming was fairly routine.

 Most of the difficulty (perhaps difficulty is the wrong word... Work?
 Non-routine excercise?) came from writing the javadocs. I've found javadocs to
 be well worth the effort in previous projects that grew to a size where
 forgetting the nuances of a method was common, but it's still in the process of
 becoming routine the same way writing code has.

 [1] https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5585779/how-do-i-convert-a-string-to-an-int-in-java
